is [Select]\n\nContent:\n\n8 g\n\nShipping Temperature:\n\nAmbient\n\nStorage Temperature:\n\nAmbient\n\nPhysical Form:\n\nPowder\n\nStability:\n\n> 2 years under recommended storage conditions\n\nCAS Number:\n\n11078-27-6\n\nSource:\n\nSugar-beet pulp\n\nPurity:\n\n> 95%\n\nMonosaccharides (%):\n\nArabinose: Galactose: Rhamnose: Galacturonic acid: Other sugars = 74.1: 13.3: 1.4: 8.3: 2.9\n\nMain Chain Glycosidic Linkage:\n\nα-1,5\n\nSubstrate For (Enzyme):\n\nendo-Arabinanase\n\nHigh purity Arabinan (Sugar Beet) for use in research, biochemical enzyme assays and analytical testing applications.\n\nSee more of our high purity polysaccharides.\n\n\n## Publications\n\nImage: Cover image for publication: Determination of total dietary fibre and available carbohydrates\n\nHydrolysis of wheat flour arabinoxylan, acid-debranched wheat flour arabinoxylan and arabino-xylo-oligosaccharides by β-xylanase, α-L-arabinofuranosidase and β-xylosidase.\n\nMcCleary, B. V., McKie, V. A., Draga, A., Rooney, E., Mangan, D. & Larkin, J. (2015). Carbohydrate Research, 407, 79-96.\n\nHydrolysis of wheat flour arabinoxylan, acid-debranched wheat flour arabinoxylan and arabino-xylo-oligosaccharides by β-xylanase, α-L-arabinofuranosidase and β-xylosidase.\n\nMcCleary, B. V., McKie, V. A., Draga, A., Rooney, E., Mangan, D. & Larkin, J. (2015). Carbohydrate Research, 407, 79-96.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNEOGEN | Megazyme (https://prod-docs.megazyme.com/documents/Data_Sheet/P-ARAB_DATA.pdf)\nciteturn32search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: 3.2 years ago; NEOGEN | Megazyme ... **CAS:** 11078-27-6 ... The upper chemical structure shows a repeating arabinan polysaccharide backbone consisting of linked sugar rings, enclosed in square brackets with a subscript *n*.\nNEOGEN | Megazyme\n\n# ARABINAN (Sugar Beet)\n\n**08/23**\n\n## P-ARAB\n\n**CAS:** 11078-27-6  \n**Source:** Sugar-beet pulp\n\n### STRUCTURE:\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nSchematic representation of Arabinan.\n\nThe upper chemical structure shows a repeating arabinan polysaccharide backbone consisting of linked sugar rings, enclosed in square brackets with a subscript *n*. The backbone contains oxygen linkages and hydroxyl (OH) groups. A blue-colored arabinofuranosyl sugar residue is shown as a branch attached to the black backbone structure. A callout on the right identifies the associated terminal structure as **Pectin fragment**.\n\nThe lower schematic shows the repeating backbone notation:\n\n**[Araα1-5Araα1-5Araα1-5]ₙ**\n\nA blue vertical branch labeled **Araα1** is attached to the backbone. The right-hand end is labeled **Pectin fragment**.\n\nThe diagram illustrates the arabinan structure and its attachment to fragments of the pectin molecule.\n</visual_element>\n\nSchematic representation of Arabinan.\n\n**Refer to the product lot number Certificate of Analysis for lot specific properties.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNEOGEN | Megazyme (https://d1kkimny8vk5e2.cloudfront.net/documents/Data_Sheet/P-ARAB_DATA.pdf)\nciteturn32search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; Crawled: 3.2 years ago; NEOGEN | Megazyme ... **CAS:** 11078-27-6 ... Chemical structure showing an arabinan polysaccharide backbone composed of repeating arabinose sugar residues, with a blue-highlighted arabinofuranosyl side-chain residue.\nNEOGEN | Megazyme\n\n# ARABINAN (Sugar Beet)\n\n**P-ARAB**\n\n**Date:** 08/23\n\n**CAS:** 11078-27-6  \n**Source:** Sugar-beet pulp\n\n## STRUCTURE\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nSchematic representation of Arabinan (sugar beet).\n\nChemical structure showing an arabinan polysaccharide backbone composed of repeating arabinose sugar residues, with a blue-highlighted arabinofuranosyl side-chain residue.\n\n- Main backbone: repeating 1,5-linked α-L-arabinofuranosyl residues.\n- Side-chain substitution: 1,3-linked α-L-arabinofuranosyl residues (and possibly some 1,2-linked residues).\n- The main chain is shown within square brackets with a repeating-unit designation n.\n- A blue-highlighted arabinofuranose residue is attached as a branch to the black backbone.\n- The reducing terminal arabinosyl residue is connected through rhamnose to fragments of the pectin backbone.\n- Two callouts in the chemical schematic are labeled **\"Pectin fragment\"**, indicating connections to the pectin structure.\n- The lower schematic shows the repeating sequence: [Araα1-5Araα1-5Araα1-5] with a blue **Araα1** branch and a repeating-unit designation n.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nARABINAN (SUGAR BEET)  (Lot 130801a) (https://libios.fr/imgfr/produit/fichier/p-arab-data.pdf)\nciteturn32search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.4 years ago; ARABINAN (SUGAR BEET)  (Lot 130801a) ... 11078-27-6\nARABINAN (SUGAR BEET)  (Lot 130801a)\nP-ARAB\t\n06/19\nCAS:\t\n11078-27-6\nSTRUCTURE\n(paste structure)\nSchematic representation of Arabinan.\nPREPARATION\nArabinan is extracted from sugar-beet pulp with calcium hydroxide solution at 90°C.  The \nrecovered arabinan is subsequently purified by precipitation and treatment with ion-exchange \nresins.  Sugar beet arabinan consists of a 1,5-α-linked backbone to which 1,3-α-linked (and \npossibly some 1,2-α-linked) L-arabinofuranosyl residues are attached.  Approximately 60% of \nthe main-chain arabinofuranosyl residues are substituted by single 1,3-linked arabinofuranosyl \ngroups.  The reducing terminal arabinosyl residue is attached through rhamnose to fragments \nof the rhamnogalacturonan backbone of the native pectin molecule.\nPROPERTIES\nSugar Composition:\t\nArabinose 74.1%, Galactose 13.3%, Rhamnose 1.4%, Galacturonic acid \n8.3%, Other sugars 2.9%\nProtein:\t\n3.2%\nAsh:\t\n5.3%\nPhysical Description:\t\noff-white, odourless powder\nSTORAGE CONDITIONS\nStore dry at room temperature in a well-sealed container.  Under these conditions, the \nproduct is stable for several years.\nDISSOLUTION:\nArabinan is readily soluble in cold water.  Arabinan concentrations of up to 20% w/v can be \nachieved in hot water.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMegazyme\n\nLINEAR I,5-α-L-ARABINAN (SUGAR BEET) (Lo (https://prod-docs.megazyme.com/documents/Data_Sheet/P-LARB_DATA.pdf)\nciteturn32search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.7 years ago; LINEAR I,5-α-L-ARABINAN (SUGAR BEET) (Lot 161001) ... CAS: 11078-27-6\nMegazyme\n\nLINEAR I,5-α-L-ARABINAN (SUGAR BEET) (Lot 161001)\n\nCAT. NO: P-LARB  03/17  \nCAS: 11078-27-6\n\nSTRUCTURE\n\nSchematic representation of Linear I,5-α-L-arabinan (sugar beet)\n\n[Araα1-5Araα1-5Araα1-5]n\n\nPREPARATION  \nLinear arabinan is prepared by ion exchange chromatography of debranched arabinan to remove most of the charged pectic fraction. The non-bound fraction is concentrated and freeze-dried.\n\nPROPERTIES\n\n| Property | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Purity: | > 95% |\n| Sugar Composition: | Arabinose 85.2%, galactose 7.6%, rhamnose 1.5%, galacturonic acid 5.7% |\n| Protein: | 1.8% |\n| Ash: | 1.3% |\n| Moisture: | 2.0% |\n| Proton Resonance NMR: | Pattern is identical to that for linear arabinan from pear juice and shows the absence of 1,3-α-linked L-arabinofuranosyl residues. |\n| Physical description: | Odourless, pure white powder. |\n| Solubility: | Limited solubility in cold water. Dissolves in water at 70°C, but will precipitate from solution on storage at 4°C. |\n\nSTORAGE CONDITIONS  \nStore dry at room temperature in a well-sealed container. Under these conditions, the product is stable for several years.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHigh molecular weight constituents from roots of Echinacea pallida: an arabinogalactan-protein and an arabinan. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/15896372/)\nciteturn32search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; # High molecular weight constituents from roots of Echinacea pallida: an arabinogalactan-protein and an arabinan. ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\nInvestigations of the neutral sugar composition revealed Gal (52.1% w/w) and Ara (38.2% w/w) in a ratio of 1.4:1, accompanied by Glc (6.9% w/w) and Rha (2.8% w/w). The content of uronic acids was 6.2%. Mild acid hydrolysis detects Ara and Glc being located at the periphery of the molecule. Linkage analyses and NMR spectroscopy revealed a backbone of the polysaccharide mainly consisting of 3-linked and 3,6-linked Galp-residues. Side chains are composed of 3,6-linked or 6-linked Galp terminating in 5-linked Araf, terminal Araf, Glcp and GlcAp. The protein part (3.9% w/w) of the AGP is rich in Hyp, Ser, Ala, Thr, Glu, Asp and Gly. The amount of Hyp was determined by a colorimetric method and found to be (0.65% (w/w) of the AGP, which is in good agreement with the result obtained by amino acid hydrolysis (0.67% w/w). The arabinan was isolated from the supernatant of the Yariv precipitation on the basis of solubility in EtOH (80%). It mainly consists of Ara (85.8%). Linkage analyses and NMR spectroscopy indicate a highly branched molecule, consisting of 3,5-linked, 5-linked and terminal Araf-residues in equal amounts.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Echinacea / chemistry\n  * Galactans / chemistry\n  * Galactans / isolation & purification\n  * Glycoproteins / chemistry\n  * Plant Proteins / chemistry\n  * Plant Roots / chemistry\n  * Polysaccharides / chemistry\n  * Polysaccharides / isolation & purification\n\n## Substances\n\n  * Galactans\n  * Glycoproteins\n  * Plant Proteins\n  * Polysaccharides\n  * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnusual linear polysaccharides: (1→5)-α-L-arabinan, (1→3)-(1→4)-α-D-glucan and (1→4)-β-D-xylan from pulp of buriti (Mauritia flexuosa), an edible palm fruit from the Amazon region. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25466005/)\nciteturn32search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.6 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # Unusual linear polysaccharides: (1→5)-α-L-arabinan, (1→3)-(1→4)-α-D-glucan and (1→4)-β-D-xylan from pulp of buriti (Mauritia flexuosa), an edible palm fruit from the Amazon region. ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n  * Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n\n## Abstract\n\nA structural characterisation of polysaccharides extracted from ripe pulp of edible buriti, an exotic tropical fruit from Mauritia flexuosa palm was carried out. After fractionation by freeze-thaw treatment of an aqueous extract (BW), a fraction containing a linear (1→5)-α-L-arabinan and a linear (1→3)-(1→4)-α-D-glucan was obtained (0.2% yield). The proportion of (1→3)- and (1→4)-linked Glcp units was 1:4. After fractionation by freeze-thaw and Fehling treatments of an alkaline extract (BK), a linear (1→4)-β-D-xylan was purified (0.6% yield). Their chemical structures were performed by sugar composition, methylation and NMR spectroscopy analysis. In addition, these results together with monosaccharide analysis of SBW and SBK are discussed in relation to literature data on polysaccharide compositions of primary cell walls of palms (commelinid monocotyledons).\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Arecaceae / chemistry\n  * Brazil\n  * Cell Wall / chemistry\n  * Fruit / chemistry\n  * Glucans / chemistry\n  * Magnetic Resonance Spectroscopy\n  * Molecular Structure\n  * Plant Extracts / chemistry\n  * Polysaccharides / chemistry\n  * Xylans / chemistry\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * (1→3)-(1→4)-α-d-Glucan\n  * (1→4)-β-d-Homoxylan\n  * (1→5)-α-l-Arabinan\n  * Arecaceae\n  * Buriti pulp\n  * Mauritia flexuosa\n\n## Substances\n\n  * Glucans\n  * Plant Extracts\n  * Polysaccharides\n  * Xylans\n  * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLINEAR 1,5-a-L-ARABINAN (SUGAR BEET)  (Lot 161001) (https://libios.fr/imgfr/produit/fichier/p-larb-data.pdf)\nciteturn32search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; LINEAR 1,5-a-L-ARABINAN (SUGAR BEET)  (Lot 161001) ... 11078-27-6\nLINEAR 1,5-a-L-ARABINAN (SUGAR BEET)  (Lot 161001)\nCAT. NO:\t\nP-LARB\t\n03/17\nCAS:\t\n11078-27-6\nSTRUCTURE\nSchematic representation of Linear 1,5-a-L-arabinan (sugar beet)\nPREPARATION\nLinear arabinan is prepared by ion exchange chromatography of debranched arabinan to \nremove most of the charged pectic fraction.  The non-bound fraction is concentrated and \nfreeze-dried.\nPROPERTIES\nPurity:\t\n> 95%\nSugar Composition:\t\nArabinose 85.2%, galactose 7.6%, rhamnose 1.5%, \ngalacturonic acid 5.7%\nProtein:\t\n1.8%\nAsh:\t\n1.3%\nMoisture:\t\n2.0%\nProton Resonance NMR:\t\nPattern is identical to that for linear arabinan from pear juice \nand shows the absence of 1,3-α-linked L-arabinofuranosyl \nresidues.\nPhysical description:\t\nOdourless, pure white powder.\nSolubility:\t\nLimited solubility in cold water.  Dissolves in water at 70°C, \nbut will precipitate from solution on storage at 4°C.\nSTORAGE CONDITIONS\nStore dry at room temperature in a well-sealed container.  Under these conditions, the \nproduct is stable for several years.\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nβ-L-Araf-containing arabinan and glucuronoxylan from guavira fruit pomace. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31220627/)\nciteturn32search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; An arabinan was purified, presenting Ara (85.0%), Rha (3.3%), Gal (7.7%) and GalA (4.0%). ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\nElectronic address: lucimaramcc@ufpr.br.\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nGuavira is a plant that belongs to Myrtaceae family, being widespread in the Brazilian Cerrado. In this study, pectic and hemicellulosic polysaccharides from guavira pomace, an agroindustry residue from pulp production, were structurally characterized using GPC, monosaccharide composition, methylation and NMR experiments. The absolute configurations of monosaccharides and the nature of uronic acids were attributed according to numerous data on the composition of related plant arabinogalactans and hemicelluloses present in the literature. An arabinan was purified, presenting Ara (85.0%), Rha (3.3%), Gal (7.7%) and GalA (4.0%). Mono and bidimensional NMR analyses of this arabinan demonstrated the presence of terminal β-L-Araf units, whose occurrence has been scarcely reported in the literature. Hemicellulosic fraction contained a glucuronoxylan, with α-D-GlcpA/4-O-methyl-α-D-GlcpA group linked to O-2 of a (1 → 4)-β-D-xylan, presenting one uronic acid residue for every six xylose units. These findings about guavira pomace polysaccharides could contribute to develop future nutraceutical and technological uses for this industrial waste.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Fruit / chemistry\n  * Myrtaceae / chemistry\n  * Polysaccharides / chemistry\n  * Polysaccharides / isolation & purification\n  * Xylans / chemistry\n  * Xylans / isolation & purification\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * Arabinan\n  * Campomanesia sp.\n  * Fruit pulp industry by-product\n  * Glucuronoxylan\n  * Guavira\n\n## Substances\n\n  * Polysaccharides\n  * Xylans\n  * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n  * glucuronoxylan (Registry number: 37317-38-7)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStructure and immunological activity of an arabinan-rich acidic polysaccharide from Atractylodes lancea (Thunb.) DC. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34973271/)\nciteturn32search5 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # Structure and immunological activity of an arabinan-rich acidic polysaccharide from Atractylodes lancea (Thunb.) ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n  * Key Laboratory of Basic and Application Research of Beiyao (Heilongjiang Univerity of Chinese Medicine), Ministry of Education, 24 Heping Road, Harbin 150040, PR China. Electronic address: yonggangxia@163.com.\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n\n## Abstract\n\nAn arabinan-rich acidic polysaccharide, named ALP4-2 ([α]20 D = +197.8 (c 1.0 mg/mL, H2O); and Mw = 5.59 × 103 g/mol), was obtained from Atractylodes lancea (Thunb.) DC. ALP4-2 is mainly comprised of Ara along with a small amount of GalA, Gal, Rha, Glc and Xyl. The structure was decorated by glycosidic linkages of α-Araf-(1→, →3)-α-Araf-(1→, →5)-α-Araf-(1→, →3,5)-α-Araf-(1→, →2,4)-α-Rhap-(1→, α-GalAp-(1→, →4)-α-GalAp-6-OMe-(1→, →4)-α-GalAp-6-OMe and β-Galp-(1→ with a ratio of 6:1:7:5:5:1:7:1:4. The structure, configuration and microstructure of ALP4-2 was proposed by comprehensive considerations of results from SEC-MALLS-RID, SEC-HRMS, GC-MS, and 1D/2D NMR spectroscopy. Except for a high methyl ester in full pectin regions, an abundant arabinan moiety is observed in ALP4-2 with highly complex and branched characteristics. The immunoactivity displayed that ALP4-2 can significantly promote phagocytosis of macrophage without cytotoxicity, and stimulate nitric oxide and cytokines (TNF-α, IL-6 and IL-10) release on RAW 264.7.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Atractylodes\n  * Pectins / chemistry\n  * Pectins / pharmacology\n  * Phagocytosis\n  * Polysaccharides / chemistry\n  * Polysaccharides / pharmacology\n\n## Keywords\n\n  * 1D/2D NMR\n  * Arabinan\n  * Atractylodes lancea\n  * Immunoactivity\n  * Methylation analysis\n\n## Substances\n\n  * Polysaccharides\n  * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n  * Pectins (Registry number: 89NA02M4RX)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nX-ray and conformational analysis of arabinan. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/9076933/)\nciteturn32search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 29.7 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # X-ray and conformational analysis of arabinan. ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n# X-ray and conformational analysis of arabinan.\n\nCarbohydrate research\n\nPMID: 9076933\n\nPublication date: 1997 Feb 20\n\nJournal abbreviation: Carbohydr Res\n\nJournal country: Netherlands\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 0043535\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 0008-6215\n\nISSN: 0008-6215\n\nVolume: 298\n\nIssue: 1-2\n\nPages: 105-15\n\n## Identifiers\n\n  * PII: S0008-6215(96)00289-3\n  * DOI: 10.1016/s0008-6215(96)00289-3\n\n## Authors\n\n  * A Radha\n  * R Chandrasekaran\n\n## Affiliations\n\n  * Whistler Center for Carbohydrate Research, Purdue University, West Lafayette, IN 47907-1160, USA.\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n  * Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n\n## Abstract\n\nFibers prepared from the enzymically debranched plant polysaccharide arabinan, containing (1-->5)-linked alpha-L-arabinofuranose residues, produce X-ray diffraction patterns containing concentric rings indicative of a high degree of structural organization. The measured unit cell dimensions and fiber density are consistent with a 2-fold helix passing through each unit cell. According to model-building techniques and energy calculations, a total of seven molecular structures (three with C2'-endo and four with C3'-endo sugars), all of pitch 8.74 A, and hence seven different crystal-packing arrangements are the most probable. While intrachain hydrogen bonds are only present in three cases, interhelix association is facilitated by hydrogen bonds in all allomorphs. Small energy differences between them suggest that easy conformational transitions may be made from one form to another. This polymorphism and the tendency to form microcrystals in the solid state might confer upon arabinan the observed fat mimetic properties.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Carbohydrate Conformation\n  * Hydrogen Bonding\n  * Models, Molecular\n  * Polysaccharides / chemistry\n  * X-Ray Diffraction\n\n## Substances\n\n  * Polysaccharides\n  * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSimple method for refining arabinan polysaccharides by alcohol extraction of the prune, Prunus domestica L. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24096650/)\nciteturn32search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # Simple method for refining arabinan polysaccharides by alcohol extraction of the prune, Prunus domestica L. ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n# Simple method for refining arabinan polysaccharides by alcohol extraction of the prune, Prunus domestica L.\n\nBioscience, biotechnology, and biochemistry\n\nPMID: 24096650\n\nPublication date: 2013\n\nArticle date: 2013 10 07\n\nPublication status: ppublish\n\nJournal abbreviation: Biosci Biotechnol Biochem\n\nJournal country: England\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 9205717\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 0916-8451\n\nISSN: 1347-6947\n\nVolume: 77\n\nIssue: 10\n\nPages: 2137-9\n\n## Identifiers\n\n  * PII: DN/JST.JSTAGE/bbb/130392\n  * DOI: 10.1271/bbb.130392\n\n## Authors\n\n  * Yukari Hara\n  * Hitomi Mizukawa\n  * Hirotaka Yamamoto\n  * Takao Ikami\n  * Koji Kato\n  * Tomio Yabe\n\n## Affiliations\n\n  * Department of Applied Life Science, Faculty of Applied Biological Sciences, Gifu University.\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n  * Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n\n## Abstract\n\nL-Arabinose is a useful sugar in the food industry. We demonstrate here simple methods for refining arabinan polysaccharides by alcohol extraction from prune, Prunus domestica L., as a source of L-arabinose. Alcohol-soluble polysaccharides were purified from a solution of prune extracted by 80% ethanol. After fractionating the polysaccharides by ion-exchange chromatography, arabinans were identified as mainly constituted by (1→5)-linked arabinofuranosyl units.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Chemical Fractionation / methods\n  * Ethanol / chemistry\n  * Polysaccharides / isolation & purification\n  * Prunus / chemistry\n\n## Substances\n\n  * Polysaccharides\n  * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n  * Ethanol (Registry number: 3K9958V90M)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nl-Arabinose production from sugar beet arabinan by immobilized endo- and exo-arabinanases from Caldicellulosiruptor saccharolyticus in a packed-bed reactor. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/22099373/)\nciteturn32search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; The immobilized endo- and exo-arabinanases from Caldicellulosiruptor saccharolyticus produced continuously an average of 16.5 gl(-1)l-arabinose from 20 gl(-1) sugar beet arabinan at pH 5.0 and 75°C for 216 h, with a productivity of 9.9 gl(-1)h(-1) and a conversion yield of 83%. ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n# l-Arabinose production from sugar beet arabinan by immobilized endo- and exo-arabinanases from Caldicellulosiruptor saccharolyticus in a packed-bed reactor.\n\nJournal of bioscience and bioengineering\n\nPMID: 22099373\n\nPublication date: 2012 Feb\n\nArticle date: 2011 11 17\n\nPublication status: ppublish\n\nJournal abbreviation: J Biosci Bioeng\n\nJournal country: Japan\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 100888800\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 1347-4421\n\nISSN: 1347-4421\n\nVolume: 113\n\nIssue: 2\n\nPages: 239-41\n\n## Identifiers\n\n  * DOI: 10.1016/j.jbiosc.2011.10.008\n  * PII: S1389-1723(11)00406-3\n\n## Authors\n\n  * Yeong-Su Kim\n  * Yu-Ri Lim\n  * Deok-Kun Oh\n\n## Affiliations\n\n  * Department of Bioscience and Biotechnology, Konkuk University, 1 Hwayang-dong, Gwangjin-gu, Seoul 143–701, Republic of Korea.\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Journal Article\n  * Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe immobilized endo- and exo-arabinanases from Caldicellulosiruptor saccharolyticus produced continuously an average of 16.5 gl(-1)l-arabinose from 20 gl(-1) sugar beet arabinan at pH 5.0 and 75°C for 216 h, with a productivity of 9.9 gl(-1)h(-1) and a conversion yield of 83%.\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n  * Arabinose / biosynthesis\n  * Beta vulgaris / chemistry\n  * Bioreactors\n  * Enzymes, Immobilized / metabolism\n  * Glycoside Hydrolases / metabolism\n  * Gram-Positive Bacteria / enzymology\n  * Hydrogen-Ion Concentration\n  * Kinetics\n  * Polysaccharides / metabolism\n  * Temperature\n\n## Substances\n\n  * Enzymes, Immobilized\n  * Polysaccharides\n  * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n  * Arabinose (Registry number: B40ROO395Z)\n  * Glycoside Hydrolases (Registry number: EC 3.2.1.-)\n  * exo-alpha-L-arabinanase (Registry number: EC 3.2.1.-)\n  * arabinan endo-1,5-alpha-L-arabinosidase (Registry number: EC 3.2.1.99)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAltering arabinans increases Arabidopsis guard cell flexibility and stomatal opening. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35675810/)\nciteturn32search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; Manipulations leading to the novel accumulation of longer-chain arabinan epitopes in guard cell walls led to an increase in the maximal pore aperture. ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTotal synthesis of the hexasaccharide arabinan domain of mycobacterial arabinogalactan. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38981322/)\nciteturn32search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.3 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; The hexasaccharide arabinan domain of Mycobacterial Arabinogalactan was provided with the versatile methodology toward β-selective arabinofuranosylation directed by B(C6F5)3, demonstrating the effectiveness of the β-arabinofuranosylation strategy. ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOXOID MANUAL PRELIMS (https://analisisavanzados.com/modules/mod_tecdata/manuales/oxoid-manual-9th-edition.pdf)\nciteturn32search17 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.4 years ago; Crawled: 19.5 years ago; Code: LP0027  \nA biologically standardised papaic digest of liver for use as a source of nutrients in microbiological culture media.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCell wall arabinan is essential for guard cell function. (https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/13130074/)\nciteturn32search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 23.1 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; # Cell wall arabinan is essential for guard cell function. ...   * araban (Registry number: 11078-27-6)\n"
